/* Generated by tools/build_ugc_expected.py from pyq/ugc/expected/2026-12-s2.md — edit the .md, not this file. */
Object.assign((window.PYQ_EXPLAIN = window.PYQ_EXPLAIN || {}), {"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-1":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"p → q ka contrapositive ¬q → ¬p (equivalent). Converse q → p, inverse ¬p → ¬q.","concept":"### Implication ke variants\n- **Conditional**: p → q\n- **Converse**: q → p\n- **Inverse**: ¬p → ¬q\n- **Contrapositive**: ¬q → ¬p ≡ p → q\n- Converse ≡ inverse (dono ek doosre ke contrapositive).\n- **Negation**: ¬(p → q) ≡ p ∧ ¬q.\n- p → q ke English forms: \"q if p\", \"p only if q\", \"q is necessary for p\", \"p is sufficient for q\", \"q unless ¬p\".","tips":"- Contrapositive = ulta + dono negate.","sol":"p = \"it rains\", q = \"ground is wet\". Contrapositive ¬q → ¬p = \"If the ground is not wet, then it does not rain.\"\n(A) converse, (B) inverse, (D) negation.\n\n**Answer: (C) If the ground is not wet, then it does not rain.**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 1.1 (Converse, contrapositive and inverse)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-2":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Index 0 se: G A T E 2 0 2 6. p + 3 → 'E'; p + 4 se \"2026\" → length 4.","concept":"### Strings in C\n- String = char array + '\\0' terminator. `sizeof(s)` = 9 (null included), `strlen(s)` = 8.\n- `p + k` → k-th character ka address; `*(p + k)` = `p[k]`.\n- `strlen` null tak gin ta (null exclude).\n- `char *t = \"abc\";` literal read-only; `char s[] = \"abc\";` modifiable copy.\n- Useful: strcpy, strcat, strcmp (0 if equal), strrev (non-standard).","tips":"- 0-based indexing yaad rakho: p + 3 = chautha character.","sol":"*(p + 3) = 'E'; strlen(\"2026\") = 4 → output **E4**.\n\n**Answer: (B) E4**","refs":"- Kernighan & Ritchie — The C Programming Language, Sec 5.5 (Character pointers and functions), Appendix B3 (<string.h>)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-3":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"M:N relationship ko foreign key se kisi ek entity me merge nahi kar sakte — alag table chahiye.","concept":"### ER → relational mapping (minimum tables)\n- Har strong entity → ek table.\n- **1:1** → kisi ek side merge (total participation wali side better) → 2 tables (dono total ho to 1 bhi).\n- **1:N** → N side me foreign key → 2 tables.\n- **M:N** → relationship ki alag table (dono primary keys + relationship attributes) → 3 tables.\n- **Weak entity** → owner ki key + partial key.\n- **Multivalued attribute** → alag table.","tips":"- M:N = hamesha extra table.","sol":"E1, E2 ke 2 tables + R (M:N) ki 1 table = **3**.\n\n**Answer: (D) 3**","refs":"- Elmasri & Navathe — Fundamentals of Database Systems, Sec 9.1 (ER-to-relational mapping algorithm).\n- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 6.7 (Reduction to relational schemas)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-4":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"6.25 = 110.01₂ = 1.1001 × 2². Sign 1, exponent 2 + 127 = 129, mantissa 1001000…","concept":"### IEEE 754 single precision (32 bits)\n- 1 sign | 8 exponent (bias 127) | 23 fraction (hidden leading 1).\n- Value = (−1)^S × 1.F × 2^(E − 127).\n- Double: 1 | 11 (bias 1023) | 52.\n- Special: E = 0, F = 0 → ±0; E = 255, F = 0 → ±∞; E = 255, F ≠ 0 → NaN; E = 0, F ≠ 0 → denormal.","tips":"- 1 10000001 1001000… → 1100 0000 1100 1000 0… = C0C8 0000.","sol":"Sign = 1; exponent = 129 = 10000001; fraction = 1001 000…\nBits: 1100 0000 1100 1000 0000 … = **C0C80000**. (40C80000 = +6.25.)\n\n**Answer: (A) C0C80000**","refs":"- William Stallings — Computer Organization and Architecture, Sec 10.4 (Floating-point representation, IEEE 754)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-5":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Pass 1: location counter + symbol table (addresses). Pass 2: symbol table use karke object code.","concept":"### Two-pass assembler (Beck, SIC)\n- **Pass 1**: LOCCTR maintain, labels ko address (SYMTAB), literals (LITTAB), directives (START, END, RESW, RESB, BYTE, WORD) process, intermediate file.\n- **Pass 2**: opcode (OPTAB) + operand address (SYMTAB) → object code; header, text, end records.\n- **Forward reference**: label use pehle, define baad me → two passes se solve. One-pass assembler → backpatching.\n- **Loader** types: absolute, relocating (modification records), linking loader; bootstrap loader.\n- **Macro processor**: macro definition table (DEFTAB), name table (NAMTAB), argument table (ARGTAB).","tips":"- Assembler: assembly → machine (ulta nahi).","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✓, (D) ✗ (pass 1 me hi), (E) ✗.\n\n**Answer: (A) (A), (B) and (C) Only**","refs":"- Leland L. Beck — System Software: An Introduction to Systems Programming, Sec 2.1 (Basic assembler functions), Sec 2.2–2.4."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-6":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"SRS batata hai system **kya** karega (what), **kaise** (how) nahi; aur coding se pehle banta hai.","concept":"### IEEE 830 — good SRS\nCorrect, unambiguous, complete, consistent, ranked for importance/stability, verifiable, modifiable, traceable.\n- SRS = black-box view; design decisions nahi.\n- Contents: introduction, overall description, specific requirements (functional, non-functional, interfaces).\n- Requirements validation: reviews, prototyping, test-case generation.","tips":"- \"Implementation detail\" aur \"after coding\" → SRS ke liye galat.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✗, (C) ✓, (D) ✓, (E) ✗.\n\n**Answer: (C) (A), (C) and (D) Only**","refs":"- IEEE Std 830-1998 — Recommended Practice for Software Requirements Specifications, Sec 4.3.\n- Rajib Mall — Fundamentals of Software Engineering, Ch 4 (Requirements analysis and specification)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-7":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Internal nodes: index 1 (20) aur index 0 (10). Pehle index 1 heapify, phir root.","concept":"### Binary heap\n- Array me: children of i (0-based) = 2i + 1, 2i + 2; parent = ⌊(i − 1)/2⌋.\n- **BUILD-MAX-HEAP**: i = ⌊n/2⌋ − 1 se 0 tak MAX-HEAPIFY → **O(n)**.\n- Insert (successive): O(log n) each → O(n log n).\n- Extract-max O(log n); heap sort O(n log n), in-place, unstable.\n- Max-heap me minimum element leaves (last ⌈n/2⌉ positions) me.","tips":"- Bottom-up aur one-by-one insertion ke results alag ho sakte — method dhyan se padho.","sol":"- i = 1 (20): children 30, 40 → swap with 40 → [10, 40, 15, 30, 20].\n- i = 0 (10): children 40, 15 → swap with 40 → [40, 10, 15, 30, 20]; 10 at index 1: children 30, 20 → swap with 30 → [40, 30, 15, 10, 20].\n\n**Answer: (D) [40, 30, 15, 10, 20]**","refs":"- Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein — Introduction to Algorithms (CLRS), Sec 6.2–6.3 (MAX-HEAPIFY, BUILD-MAX-HEAP)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-8":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"(a + b)* = sab strings. Check karo \"ba\" kaun generate kar sakta hai.","concept":"### Regular expression identities\n- (a + b)* = (a*b*)* = (a*b)*a* = (b*a)*b* = (a* + b*)*.\n- a* + b* ≠ (a + b)* (\"ab\" missing).\n- a*b* ≠ (a + b)* (\"ba\" missing).\n- (r*)* = r*, r*r* = r*, (ε + r)* = r*, ∅* = ε.\n- Arden's theorem: R = Q + RP → R = QP* (agar P me ε na ho).","tips":"- Counter-example string \"ba\" se A, B, D teeno fail.","sol":"(a*b*)*: a*b* me a aur b dono (ε bhi) → star se koi bhi sequence → **(a + b)***. Baaki \"ba\" nahi bana sakte.\n\n**Answer: (C) (a*b*)***","refs":"- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation, Sec 3.4 (Algebraic laws for regular expressions)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-9":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Har bit ke beech me transition → har bit do signal elements.","concept":"### Line coding\n- **NRZ-L / NRZ-I**: baud = bit rate (worst case); long 0/1 runs me sync problem.\n- **RZ**: 3 levels, 2 signal elements per bit.\n- **Manchester** (Ethernet 10 Mbps): mid-bit transition = bit value + clock; baud = 2 × bit rate; no DC component.\n- **Differential Manchester** (Token Ring): bit ke start pe transition = 0.\n- **AMI / bipolar**: 0 → zero voltage, 1 → alternate +/−.\n- **4B/5B, 8B/10B**: block coding (redundancy for sync).\n- Modulation: ASK, FSK, PSK, QAM (bits per symbol = log₂ levels).","tips":"- Self-clocking ka price = double bandwidth.","sol":"Manchester me har bit ke liye 2 signal elements → baud rate = **2 × bit rate**.\n\n**Answer: (D) Two times**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Sec 4.1 (Line coding schemes: polar biphase Manchester)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-10":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"MIN1 = 6 → root ka α = 6. Kisi MIN node me koi leaf ≤ 6 mila to us node ke baaki leaves cut.","concept":"### Minimax & alpha-beta\n- **Minimax**: MAX levels pe max, MIN levels pe min; complete tree O(b^m).\n- **α** = MAX ke liye ab tak ka best (lower bound); **β** = MIN ke liye ab tak ka best (upper bound).\n- Prune jab α ≥ β.\n- Best move ordering → O(b^(m/2)) (same time me depth double).\n- Pruning se root ki final value nahi badalti.","tips":"- MIN node me leaf α se **bada** ho to cut nahi — aage padhna padta (MIN2 ka 8).","sol":"- MIN1 = min(6, 9, 7) = 6 → α = 6.\n- MIN2: 8 (> 6, continue), 4 → MIN2 ≤ 4 ≤ α → leaf 10 pruned (1).\n- MIN3: 5 → MIN3 ≤ 5 ≤ α → leaves 12, 3 pruned (2).\nTotal pruned = **3**; root value = 6.\n\n**Answer: (D) 3**","refs":"- Russell & Norvig — Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach, Sec 5.2–5.3 (Minimax, alpha-beta pruning)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-11":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Equivalence relations ↔ partitions. 3 elements ke partitions = Bell number B₃.","concept":"### Equivalence relations\n- Reflexive + symmetric + transitive.\n- Har equivalence relation ek **partition** banata (equivalence classes) aur ulta.\n- Count = **Bell number**: B₀ = 1, B₁ = 1, B₂ = 2, B₃ = 5, B₄ = 15, B₅ = 52.\n- Stirling number S(n, k) = n elements ke k non-empty blocks; Bₙ = Σ S(n, k).\n- Smallest equivalence relation = identity (diagonal); largest = A × A.","tips":"- {a,b,c}: {abc}, {a|bc}, {b|ac}, {c|ab}, {a|b|c} → 5.","sol":"Partitions of {a, b, c}: 1 (ek block) + 3 (do blocks) + 1 (teen blocks) = **5**.\n\n**Answer: (A) 5**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 9.5 (Equivalence relations and partitions)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-12":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Keys: AB aur BD (D → A se). D → A me D superkey nahi par A prime → 3NF ok, BCNF fail.","concept":"### 3NF vs BCNF\n- **3NF**: X → A me X superkey **ya** A prime.\n- **BCNF**: X → A me X superkey (koi exception nahi).\n- Overlapping candidate keys (AB, BD me B common) aksar \"3NF but not BCNF\" dete hain.\n- BCNF decomposition yahan: (D, A) aur (B, C, D) → lossless, par AB → CD preserve nahi hota.","tips":"- \"Prime attribute RHS pe\" = 3NF ka exception.","sol":"B kabhi RHS me nahi → har key me B.\n- AB → CD → poora R ✓ key.\n- BD → (D → A) → ABD → AB → CD ✓ key.\nPrime = A, B, D; non-prime = C.\n- AB → CD: AB key ✓ (2NF, 3NF, BCNF sab ok).\n- D → A: D superkey nahi ✗ BCNF; A prime ✓ 3NF.\nHighest = **3NF**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 3NF**","refs":"- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 7.3 (BCNF and 3NF comparison).\n- Elmasri & Navathe — Fundamentals of Database Systems, Sec 14.5."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-13":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"D copy karta, T toggle karta (XOR), JK me J set, K reset, dono 1 = toggle.","concept":"### Flip-flops\n- **SR**: S = R = 1 invalid (NOR latch).\n- **JK**: J = K = 1 → toggle; race-around (level-triggered) → master-slave / edge-triggered.\n- **D**: data/delay; register banane me.\n- **T**: toggle; counters me.\n- Excitation tables (Q → Q⁺): JK: 0→0 (0,X), 0→1 (1,X), 1→0 (X,1), 1→1 (X,0).\n- Conversion: JK se T (J = K = T), JK se D (J = D, K = D').","tips":"- JK ka equation SR jaisa hai par toggle ke saath — J Q' + K' Q.","sol":"D → III, T → IV, JK → I, SR → II.\n\n**Answer: (B) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Digital Design, Sec 5.4 (Flip-flops, characteristic equations), Sec 5.7."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-14":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Spiral ke 4 quadrants (clockwise): objectives → risk analysis → engineering (develop/test) → planning next.","concept":"### Spiral model (Boehm, 1986)\n- **Meta-model**: har loop ek phase; radius = cumulative cost, angle = progress.\n- Quadrant 1: objectives, alternatives, constraints.\n- Quadrant 2: **risk analysis** (prototyping se risks resolve) — model ki pehchaan.\n- Quadrant 3: develop & verify (design, code, test).\n- Quadrant 4: customer review + next loop ki planning.\n- Bade, high-risk projects ke liye; chhote projects ke liye mehenga.\n- **WINWIN spiral**: har loop ki shuruaat me stakeholders ke saath negotiation.","tips":"- Risk analysis hamesha develop karne se **pehle**.","sol":"A (objectives) → B (risk analysis) → C (develop/verify) → D (plan next).\n\n**Answer: (D) (A), (B), (C), (D)**","refs":"- Barry Boehm — \"A Spiral Model of Software Development and Enhancement\", IEEE Computer, 1988.\n- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Sec 4.1.3 (Evolutionary process models: the spiral model)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-15":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"case 2 se entry; break na ho to aage ke cases bhi chalte (fall-through) jab tak break na mile.","concept":"### switch in C\n- Expression integer/char type; case labels constant, unique.\n- Match wale case se execution shuru, **break** tak (fall-through).\n- default kahin bhi likh sakte; koi case match na ho tab.\n- `continue` switch me loop ke liye hota (switch akela continue support nahi karta).","tips":"- break ke baad wala default kabhi nahi chalega (yahan).","sol":"x = 2 → \"B\" → fall-through → \"C\" → break. Output **BC**.\n\n**Answer: (C) BC**","refs":"- Kernighan & Ritchie — The C Programming Language, Sec 3.4 (Switch)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-16":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"|A ∪ B| = ⌊1000/3⌋ + ⌊1000/5⌋ − ⌊1000/15⌋.","concept":"### Inclusion–exclusion\n- |A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| − |A ∩ B|.\n- Divisible by a and b both → divisible by lcm(a, b).\n- Teen sets: |A ∪ B ∪ C| = Σ|A| − Σ|A ∩ B| + |A ∩ B ∩ C|.\n- \"Neither\" = total − |A ∪ B| (yahan 1000 − 467 = 533 — option trap).\n- Euler phi: φ(n) = n Π (1 − 1/p).","tips":"- ⌊1000/3⌋ = 333, ⌊1000/5⌋ = 200, ⌊1000/15⌋ = 66.","sol":"333 + 200 − 66 = **467**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 467**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 8.5 (Inclusion–exclusion)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-17":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"AES symmetric; RSA factoring; SHA hash; DH key exchange.","concept":"### Cryptography\n- **Symmetric**: DES (64-bit block, 56-bit key, 16 rounds, Feistel), 3DES, **AES** (128-bit block; 128/192/256-bit key; 10/12/14 rounds; SPN), Blowfish, RC4 (stream).\n- **Asymmetric**: RSA (factoring), ElGamal & DH (discrete log), ECC (elliptic curve discrete log).\n- **Hash**: MD5 (128-bit), SHA-1 (160), SHA-256 (256); properties: one-way, collision resistance.\n- **MAC/HMAC**: hash + secret key → integrity + authentication.\n- **Digital signature**: sender ke private key se sign → authentication, integrity, non-repudiation.\n- n users: symmetric keys n(n − 1)/2; asymmetric 2n.","tips":"- DH sirf key exchange karta, encryption nahi; man-in-the-middle ke against authentication chahiye.","sol":"AES → III, RSA → IV, SHA-256 → II, DH → I.\n\n**Answer: (A) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)**","refs":"- William Stallings — Cryptography and Network Security, Ch 6 (AES), Ch 9 (RSA), Ch 10 (Diffie-Hellman), Ch 11 (Hash functions)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-18":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Ratios: 5, 4, 3. Item 1 aur 2 poore (weight 30), bachi capacity 10 me item 3 ka 10/25 hissa.","concept":"### Knapsack\n- **Fractional**: greedy by v/w ratio → optimal; O(n log n).\n- **0/1**: greedy fail ho sakta → DP O(nW).\n- Isi data pe 0/1 optimum = 130 (items 1 + 2); fractional hamesha ≥ 0/1.\n- Fractional me sirf ek hi item tukde me aata (last wala).","tips":"- 10/25 × 75 = 30.","sol":"Item 1 (50) + item 2 (80) = 130, weight 30. Bachi capacity 10 → item 3 ka 10/25 → 0.4 × 75 = 30.\nTotal = **160**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 160**","refs":"- Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein — Introduction to Algorithms (CLRS), Sec 16.2 (Elements of the greedy strategy: fractional vs 0-1 knapsack)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-19":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Thread = lightweight process; same address space → switch sasta.","concept":"### Threads\n- Shared: code, data, heap, open files, signals. Private: thread ID, PC, registers, **stack**.\n- **User-level threads**: library manage karti, fast switch, kernel ko pata nahi; ek blocking call poora process block (many-to-one).\n- **Kernel-level threads**: OS manage karta; ek block ho to doosre chal sakte; switch costly (thoda).\n- Models: many-to-one, one-to-one (Linux, Windows), many-to-many.\n- Benefits: responsiveness, resource sharing, economy, multicore scalability.","tips":"- \"Kernel-level threads invisible to OS\" → ulta (user-level invisible).","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✓, (D) ✗, (E) ✗.\n\n**Answer: (B) (A), (B) and (C) Only**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Ch 4 (Threads: multithreading models)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-20":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"wwᴿ me beech kahan hai pata nahi → guess karna padta (non-determinism). aⁿbⁿ deterministic hai.","concept":"### DCFL vs CFL\n- **DCFL**: aⁿbⁿ, wcwᴿ (centre marker), balanced parentheses — DPDA se.\n- **CFL, not DCFL**: wwᴿ (even palindromes), {aⁿbⁿ} ∪ {aⁿb²ⁿ}.\n- DCFL complement ke under closed; unambiguous grammar hota.\n- aⁿbⁿcⁿ → CSL (LBA). (a + b)*abb → regular (DFA, 4 states).","tips":"- Centre marker \"c\" ho to deterministic, na ho to non-deterministic.","sol":"A → DPDA (III), B → NPDA (I), C → LBA (IV), D → DFA (II).\n\n**Answer: (D) (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)**","refs":"- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation, Sec 6.4 (Deterministic PDAs)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-21":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Minsky → frames; Schank → conceptual dependency aur scripts; Quillian → semantic nets.","concept":"### Knowledge representation schemes\n- **Semantic network** (Quillian): IS-A, HAS-A links; inheritance.\n- **Frames** (Minsky, 1975): slots, default values, procedural attachments (if-needed, if-added).\n- **Scripts** (Schank & Abelson): events ka standard sequence (restaurant script: entry, ordering, eating, exit).\n- **Conceptual dependency** (Schank): primitive acts — ATRANS, PTRANS, MTRANS, INGEST …\n- **Production rules**: expert systems; forward chaining (data-driven), backward chaining (goal-driven).\n- **Logic**: propositional, FOL (resolution), non-monotonic reasoning.","tips":"- Naam–person pairing se jaldi elimination.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✓, (D) ✓, (E) ✗ (Roger Schank).\n\n**Answer: (A) (A), (B), (C) and (D) Only**","refs":"- Rich & Knight — Artificial Intelligence, Ch 9–10 (Weak and strong slot-and-filler structures: semantic nets, frames, CD, scripts)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-22":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Klein 4-group abelian hai par cyclic nahi. Zₙ ke generators = φ(n).","concept":"### Group results\n- Cyclic ⇒ abelian (aᵐaⁿ = aⁿaᵐ); ulta false (Klein V₄ = Z₂ × Z₂).\n- **Lagrange**: |H| divides |G|. Converse false (A₄ ke order 12 me order 6 subgroup nahi).\n- Prime order p → cyclic, har non-identity element generator.\n- (Zₙ, +) ke generators: gcd(k, n) = 1 wale k → φ(n). φ(6) = 2 (1 aur 5).\n- Subgroup test: H non-empty, a, b ∈ H ⇒ ab⁻¹ ∈ H.","tips":"- \"Every abelian is cyclic\" — classic false.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✗, (C) ✓, (D) ✓, (E) ✗ (sirf 2 generators: 1, 5).\n\n**Answer: (C) (A), (C) and (D) Only**","refs":"- I. N. Herstein — Topics in Algebra, Sec 2.4 (Lagrange's theorem), Sec 2.3.\n- C. L. Liu — Elements of Discrete Mathematics, Ch 11 (Groups)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-23":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"COUNT(column) aur AVG NULL ko ignore karte; COUNT(*) sab rows ginta.","concept":"### NULL in SQL\n- Aggregates (SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX, COUNT(col)) NULL ignore karte; COUNT(*) nahi.\n- Comparison with NULL → UNKNOWN (3-valued logic); `WHERE a = NULL` kuch nahi deta → `IS NULL` use karo.\n- NULL + 5 = NULL.\n- UNKNOWN AND FALSE = FALSE; UNKNOWN OR TRUE = TRUE.\n- GROUP BY me saare NULL ek group.","tips":"- AVG = SUM / COUNT(a), COUNT(*) se nahi: 9/3.","sol":"COUNT(a) = 3; COUNT(*) = 5; AVG(a) = (1 + 3 + 5)/3 = 3.\n\n**Answer: (D) 3, 5, 3**","refs":"- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 3.6 (Null values), Sec 3.7.4 (Aggregation with null values)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-24":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Tree: 8 + 4 + 2 + 1.","concept":"### Multiplexer facts\n- 2ⁿ:1 MUX → n select lines.\n- k:1 MUX from 2:1 MUXes → k − 1.\n- 16:1 from 4:1 MUXes → 4 + 1 = 5.\n- n-variable function → 2ⁿ:1 MUX directly, ya 2ⁿ⁻¹:1 MUX (ek variable data inputs pe).\n- Demultiplexer = decoder with enable.","tips":"- Har 2:1 MUX ek input \"kam\" karta → 16 se 1 → 15.","sol":"Levels: 8 + 4 + 2 + 1 = **15**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 15**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Digital Design, Sec 4.11 (Multiplexers)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-25":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Ek variable: min, min+, nom, max−, max (5). n variables: 4n + 1.","concept":"### Boundary value analysis variants\n- **BVA** (single fault): 4n + 1.\n- **Robust BVA** (min− aur max+ bhi): 6n + 1.\n- **Worst-case BVA**: 5ⁿ. **Robust worst-case**: 7ⁿ.\n- Equivalence class partitioning: valid + invalid classes; har class se ek test.","tips":"- n = 3 → 4·3 + 1 = 13; robust hota to 19.","sol":"4 × 3 + 1 = **13**.\n\n**Answer: (A) 13**","refs":"- Paul C. Jorgensen — Software Testing: A Craftsman's Approach, Ch 5 (Boundary value testing)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-26":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Inheritance mode = maximum visibility. Base ke private kabhi directly accessible nahi.","concept":"### Access after inheritance (base ke public, protected, private members)\n- public inheritance: public, protected, not accessible\n- protected inheritance: protected, protected, not accessible\n- private inheritance: private, private, not accessible\n- Default: `class` me private inheritance, `struct` me public.\n- Multiple inheritance + diamond problem → **virtual base class**.","tips":"- Private member inherit hota (memory me hai) par access nahi — base ke public/protected functions se hi.","sol":"A → III, B → IV, C → I, D → II.\n\n**Answer: (B) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)**","refs":"- Bjarne Stroustrup — The C++ Programming Language, Sec 20.5 (Access control, access to base classes).\n- E. Balagurusamy — Object Oriented Programming with C++, Ch 8 (Inheritance)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-27":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"N(h) = N(h − 1) + N(h − 2) + 1; N(0) = 1, N(1) = 2.","concept":"### AVL tree\n- Balance factor = h(left) − h(right) ∈ {−1, 0, 1}.\n- Rotations: LL (right rotate), RR (left rotate), LR, RL (double).\n- Height = O(log n) (≈ 1.44 log₂ n).\n- Minimum nodes: N(h) = N(h−1) + N(h−2) + 1 → 1, 2, 4, 7, 12, 20, 33 …\n- Maximum nodes height h: 2^(h+1) − 1.\n- Height convention (single node 0 ya 1) dhyan se padho.","tips":"- Height 1-based ho (single node = 1) to answer N = 7 hota — convention padho.","sol":"N(0) = 1, N(1) = 2, N(2) = 4, N(3) = 7, N(4) = 7 + 4 + 1 = **12**.\n\n**Answer: (D) 12**","refs":"- Mark Allen Weiss — Data Structures and Algorithm Analysis, Sec 4.4 (AVL trees)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-28":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"`>=` aur `+=` ek-ek token hain (do characters ke operators); whitespace token nahi.","concept":"### Lexical analysis\n- Token classes: keywords, identifiers, constants, string literals, operators, punctuators (separators).\n- Whitespace aur comments token nahi (discard).\n- Multi-character operators (`>=`, `+=`, `++`, `->`, `&&`) — **longest match** rule se ek token.\n- **Lexeme** = actual characters; **pattern** = rule (regex).\n- Lex/Flex: regex → NFA → DFA (longest match, first rule priority).\n- Lexical errors: illegal character, unterminated string.","tips":"- `>=` ko do tokens (`>` aur `=`) gin loge to 14 aayega — option trap.","sol":"Tokens: `if`(1) `(`(2) `x`(3) `>=`(4) `10`(5) `)`(6) `y`(7) `+=`(8) `x`(9) `*`(10) `2`(11) `;`(12) → **12**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 12**","refs":"- Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman — Compilers (Dragon Book), Sec 3.1 (Role of the lexical analyzer: tokens, patterns, lexemes), Sec 3.3."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-29":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Atomicity = all-or-nothing (undo via log); Consistency = programmer + constraints; Isolation = concurrency control; Durability = recovery (redo, stable storage).","concept":"### ACID (Korth)\n- **Atomicity**: partial transaction ka effect nahi — recovery system (log, undo).\n- **Consistency**: transaction akela chale to DB consistent rahe — programmer ki zimmedari.\n- **Isolation**: concurrent execution serial jaisa — concurrency-control (locks, timestamps).\n- **Durability**: commit ke baad crash pe bhi bacha rahe — recovery manager (redo, WAL).\n- Transaction states: active → partially committed → committed; failed → aborted.","tips":"- \"Programmer\" dikhe → consistency.","sol":"A → III, B → IV, C → I, D → II.\n\n**Answer: (A) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)**","refs":"- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 17.1–17.4 (Transaction concept, ACID, who ensures each)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-30":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"C = B log₂(1 + SNR) = 3000 × log₂ 64.","concept":"### Shannon capacity\n- C = B log₂(1 + SNR) — noisy channel ki theoretical upper limit.\n- SNR (dB) = 10 log₁₀(SNR); 30 dB = 1000.\n- Nyquist (noiseless): C = 2B log₂ L. Dono saath: Shannon se upper limit, Nyquist se levels L.\n- Example: L chahiye? 2B log₂ L = C → L = 2^(C/2B).","tips":"- SNR + 1 = 64 = 2⁶ → log 6.","sol":"C = 3000 × log₂(64) = 3000 × 6 = **18000 bps**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 18000 bps**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Sec 3.5.2 (Noisy channel: Shannon capacity)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-31":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Bayes: P(I | R) = P(I)P(R | I) / [P(I)P(R | I) + P(II)P(R | II)].","concept":"### Conditional probability & Bayes\n- P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B).\n- **Total probability**: P(B) = Σ P(Aᵢ)P(B | Aᵢ).\n- **Bayes**: P(Aᵢ | B) = P(Aᵢ)P(B | Aᵢ) / P(B).\n- Independent events: P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B).\n- Expected value E[X] = Σ x·P(x); binomial mean np, variance np(1 − p).","tips":"- Equal priors (1/2, 1/2) → seedha likelihoods ka ratio: 3/5 : 1/5 → 3 : 1.","sol":"P(R | I) = 3/5, P(R | II) = 1/5.\nP(I | R) = (½ × 3/5) / (½ × 3/5 + ½ × 1/5) = (3/10) / (4/10) = **3/4**.\n\n**Answer: (A) 3/4**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 7.3 (Bayes' theorem)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-32":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Memory references gino: immediate 0, direct 1, indirect 2, register indirect 1.","concept":"### Addressing modes\n- **Immediate**: operand instruction me (`MOV R1, #5`); fastest, constant ke liye.\n- **Direct / absolute**: EA = address field.\n- **Indirect**: EA = M[address field] (pointer).\n- **Register**: operand register me. **Register indirect**: EA = register content.\n- **Displacement**: EA = address field + register — **relative** (PC), **base** (base register), **indexed** (index register, arrays).\n- **Autoincrement / autodecrement**: register indirect + register update (stack, arrays).","tips":"- \"Indirect\" = pointer ka pointer → ek extra memory access.","sol":"Immediate → II, Direct → IV, Indirect → I, Register indirect → III.\n\n**Answer: (C) (A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Computer System Architecture, Sec 8.5 (Addressing modes).\n- William Stallings — Computer Organization and Architecture, Sec 13.1."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-33":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Bottom-up table banao: g(0) = 0, g(1) = 1, g(n) = g(n−1) + 2g(n−2).","concept":"### Recursion tracing tips\n- Bade recursive call tree ki jagah **bottom-up table** se values nikaalo (DP jaisa).\n- Ye recurrence Jacobsthal numbers deta: 0, 1, 1, 3, 5, 11, 21, 43 …\n- Number of calls bhi puchha ja sakta: C(n) = 1 + C(n−1) + C(n−2).\n- Tail recursion: last operation recursive call → loop me convert ho sakta.","tips":"- Base case dhyan se: n ≤ 1 → n (yaani g(0) = 0, g(1) = 1).","sol":"g(0) = 0, g(1) = 1, g(2) = 1 + 0 = 1, g(3) = 1 + 2 = 3, g(4) = 3 + 2 = 5, g(5) = 5 + 6 = **11**.\n\n**Answer: (A) 11**","refs":"- Kernighan & Ritchie — The C Programming Language, Sec 4.10 (Recursion)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-34":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Har option ka LHS closure nikaalo. D⁺ = {D} hi rehta.","concept":"### Armstrong's axioms\n- **Reflexivity**: Y ⊆ X ⇒ X → Y.\n- **Augmentation**: X → Y ⇒ XZ → YZ.\n- **Transitivity**: X → Y, Y → Z ⇒ X → Z.\n- Derived: union, decomposition, pseudo-transitivity (X → Y, WY → Z ⇒ WX → Z).\n- **Test**: X → Y implied ⇔ Y ⊆ X⁺.\n- Sound + complete.","tips":"- Closure method sabse fast: D⁺ me E nahi → D → E implied nahi.","sol":"- AD⁺ = {A, D, B, C, E} → AD → E ✓, AD → C ✓.\n- A⁺ = {A, B, C} → A → C ✓.\n- D⁺ = {D} → D → E ✗ (**not implied**).\n\n**Answer: (C) D → E**","refs":"- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 7.4.1 (Closure of a set of FDs, Armstrong's axioms)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-35":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Macro = assembly/pre-processing time pe text expansion; subroutine = run time pe CALL/RET.","concept":"### Macro vs subroutine\n- **Macro**: macro processor har invocation ko body se replace karta → code size ↑, speed ↑ (koi call overhead nahi), parameters textual.\n- **Subroutine**: code ek baar, CALL/RET; stack/return address overhead; code size kam.\n- Macro processor data structures: **DEFTAB** (definitions), **NAMTAB** (names + pointers), **ARGTAB** (arguments).\n- Features: nested macros, conditional macro expansion, keyword parameters, unique labels (`$`).","tips":"- \"Expanded\" = macro, \"called\" = subroutine.","sol":"(A) sahi — macro inline expand → size ↑, call/return overhead nahi. Baaki sab ulte statements.\n\n**Answer: (A) A macro is expanded at every point of call, so it increases program size but avoids call/return overhead.**","refs":"- Leland L. Beck — System Software: An Introduction to Systems Programming, Ch 4 (Macro processors)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-36":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Availability = MTTF / (MTTF + MTTR) = MTTF / MTBF.","concept":"### Reliability metrics\n- **MTBF** = MTTF + MTTR.\n- **Availability** = MTTF / (MTTF + MTTR).\n- **ROCOF** (rate of occurrence of failure), **POFOD** (probability of failure on demand).\n- Reliability R(t) = e^(−λt) for constant failure rate λ = 1/MTTF.\n- Series system: R = R₁R₂…; parallel: R = 1 − Π(1 − Rᵢ).","tips":"- 450 / 500 = 0.9.","sol":"Availability = 450 / (450 + 50) = 450 / 500 = **90%**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 90%**","refs":"- Ian Sommerville — Software Engineering, Sec 11.2 (Availability and reliability metrics).\n- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Sec 21.6 (Software reliability)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-37":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Inorder successor = right subtree ka minimum = 60 (leaf). 60 root pe aata, 70 ka left khaali ho jaata.","concept":"### BST deletion\n1. Leaf → seedha hatao.\n2. Ek child → child ko upar le aao.\n3. Do children → **inorder successor** (right subtree ka min) ya **predecessor** (left subtree ka max) se replace, phir use delete.\n- Search/insert/delete O(h); h worst O(n) (skewed), average O(log n).\n- Inorder traversal BST me sorted order deta.\n- n distinct keys se distinct BSTs = Catalan number C(n) = (2n)! / ((n + 1)! n!).","tips":"- Predecessor use karte to root 40 hota — option B trap.","sol":"Tree: 50 (30 (20, 40), 70 (60, 80)). Delete 50 → successor 60 → root 60, 70 ka left NULL.\nPreorder: 60, 30, 20, 40, 70, 80.\n\n**Answer: (A) 60, 30, 20, 40, 70, 80**","refs":"- Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein — Introduction to Algorithms (CLRS), Sec 12.3 (Insertion and deletion in BST)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-38":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"CFL: union ✓, intersection ✗, complement ✗. DCFL: complement ✓. RE: complement ✗ (warna har RE recursive ho jaata).","concept":"### Closure summary (union, intersection, complement, concatenation, star)\n- Regular: ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓\n- DCFL: ✗ ✗ ✓ ✗ ✗\n- CFL: ✓ ✗ ✗ ✓ ✓\n- CSL: ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓\n- Recursive: ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓\n- RE: ✓ ✓ ✗ ✓ ✓\n- CFL ∩ Regular = CFL. L aur L' dono RE ⇔ L recursive.","tips":"- Ye table ek baar likh ke yaad kar lo — har paper me 1–2 sawaal.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✗, (C) ✓, (D) ✓, (E) ✗.\n\n**Answer: (D) (A), (C) and (D) Only**","refs":"- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation, Sec 4.2, 7.3, 9.2.\n- Peter Linz — An Introduction to Formal Languages and Automata, Sec 8.2."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-39":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"UDP header 8 bytes, TCP header minimum 20 bytes. UDP me koi flow/congestion control nahi.","concept":"### TCP vs UDP\n- **TCP**: connection (3-way handshake), reliable, in-order, flow control (rwnd), congestion control (cwnd), header 20–60 bytes.\n- **UDP**: connectionless, unreliable, 8-byte header, low overhead (DNS, VoIP, streaming, SNMP, DHCP).\n### TCP congestion control\n- **Slow start**: cwnd = 1 MSS, har ACK pe +1 → har RTT double, ssthresh tak.\n- **Congestion avoidance**: har RTT +1 MSS (additive increase).\n- **Timeout**: ssthresh = cwnd/2, cwnd = 1 (Tahoe aur Reno dono).\n- **3 duplicate ACKs**: Reno → fast retransmit + fast recovery (cwnd = ssthresh); Tahoe → cwnd = 1.","tips":"- 8 bytes = UDP; 20 bytes = TCP / IPv4 minimum header.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✗, (C) ✓, (D) ✓, (E) ✗ (20 bytes).\n\n**Answer: (A) (A), (C) and (D) Only**","refs":"- Kurose & Ross — Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach, Sec 3.3 (UDP), Sec 3.5–3.7 (TCP, congestion control)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-40":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"BFS memory-hungry; DFS memory-cheap par incomplete; IDS dono ka best; bidirectional dono taraf se aadha-aadha.","concept":"### Uninformed search (b = branching factor, d = shallowest goal depth, m = max depth)\n- **BFS**: complete (b finite), optimal (unit cost), time & space O(b^d).\n- **Uniform cost**: optimal for any positive costs; O(b^(1 + ⌊C*/ε⌋)).\n- **DFS**: space O(bm); not complete (infinite depth / loops), not optimal.\n- **Depth-limited**: DFS with limit l.\n- **IDS**: complete, optimal (unit cost), time O(b^d), space O(bd) — preferred uninformed method.\n- **Bidirectional**: O(b^(d/2)); predecessors nikal sakne chahiye.","tips":"- \"O(bm) memory\" = DFS ki pehchaan.","sol":"A → II, B → IV, C → III, D → I.\n\n**Answer: (B) (A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)**","refs":"- Russell & Norvig — Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach, Sec 3.4 (Uninformed search strategies, Fig 3.21)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-41":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Odd cycle → 3 colours. Wheel = rim (C₅ → 3) + hub (sab se adjacent → 1 aur) = 4.","concept":"### Chromatic numbers\n- Kₙ → n. Empty graph → 1.\n- Cₙ: n even → 2, n odd → 3.\n- Tree / any bipartite graph (≥ 1 edge) → 2.\n- Wheel Wₙ (hub + n-cycle): n even → 3, n odd → 4.\n- Planar → ≤ 4 (Four colour theorem).\n- χ(G) ≥ clique size ω(G); χ(G) ≤ Δ + 1 (Brooks: ≤ Δ unless complete/odd cycle).\n- Chromatic polynomial: Kₙ → k(k−1)…(k−n+1); tree with n vertices → k(k−1)^(n−1).","tips":"- Wheel ka rim odd ho → 4 colours.","sol":"K₅ → 5 (III); C₇ → 3 (IV); tree → 2 (I); W₅ → 3 + 1 = 4 (II).\n\n**Answer: (D) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 10.8 (Graph coloring).\n- Narsingh Deo — Graph Theory with Applications to Engineering and Computer Science, Ch 8."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-42":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Hit: 10 ns. Miss: 10 + 100 = 110 ns. Weighted average.","concept":"### Average memory access time\n- **Hierarchical (look-through)**: T = h·Tc + (1 − h)(Tc + Tm) = Tc + (1 − h)Tm.\n- **Simultaneous (look-aside)**: T = h·Tc + (1 − h)Tm.\n- AMAT = hit time + miss rate × miss penalty.\n- Multi-level: AMAT = T₁ + m₁(T₂ + m₂ × Tmem).\n- With TLB: EAT = h(t + m) + (1 − h)(t + 2m) (single-level page table).","tips":"- Simultaneous maante to 19 ns — question ki wording dhyan se padho.","sol":"T = 0.9 × 10 + 0.1 × (10 + 100) = 9 + 11 = **20 ns**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 20 ns**","refs":"- Hennessy & Patterson — Computer Organization and Design, Sec 5.4 (Measuring and improving cache performance).\n- William Stallings — Computer Organization and Architecture, Sec 4.1."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-43":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Deferred update = NO-UNDO/REDO; immediate = UNDO/REDO; checkpoint recovery ko **fast** karta; shadow paging me purane pages se hi undo ho jaata.","concept":"### Recovery techniques\n- **Log-based**: <Ti start>, <Ti, X, old, new>, <Ti commit>.\n- **Deferred update**: DB me changes commit ke baad → crash pe sirf REDO (committed).\n- **Immediate update**: changes commit se pehle bhi → UNDO (uncommitted) + REDO (committed).\n- **Checkpoint**: us point se pehle committed transactions ko redo karne ki zaroorat nahi → recovery fast.\n- **Shadow paging**: current + shadow page table; commit = pointer switch; no log needed (single user), par fragmentation, garbage collection.\n- **ARIES**: WAL, steal/no-force, LSNs; phases: analysis, redo, undo.","tips":"- Steal → UNDO chahiye; No-force → REDO chahiye.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✓, (D) ✗, (E) ✗.\n\n**Answer: (D) (A), (B) and (C) Only**","refs":"- Korth — Database System Concepts, Ch 19 (Recovery system: log-based recovery, checkpoints, shadow paging, ARIES).\n- Elmasri & Navathe — Fundamentals of Database Systems, Ch 22."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-44":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Kaunsa function chalega ye compiler arguments dekh ke decide karta (early binding).","concept":"### Polymorphism in C++\n- **Compile-time (static, early binding)**: function overloading, operator overloading, templates.\n- **Run-time (dynamic, late binding)**: virtual functions + base class pointer/reference (vtable, vptr).\n- Overloading: same naam, alag parameter list (return type akela kaafi nahi).\n- Overriding: derived class me same signature ka virtual function.\n- Name mangling se overloaded functions ke alag symbols bante.","tips":"- \"Virtual\" dikhe → run-time; \"overloading/template\" → compile-time.","sol":"Overloading ka resolution compile time pe → **compile-time polymorphism**.\n\n**Answer: (C) Compile-time (static) polymorphism**","refs":"- Bjarne Stroustrup — The C++ Programming Language, Sec 12.3 (Overloaded functions), Sec 20.3 (Virtual functions)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-45":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Paging = fixed-size → internal fragmentation; segmentation = variable size → external.","concept":"### Memory management\n- **Paging**: no external fragmentation; average internal fragmentation = half page per process.\n- **Segmentation**: user view (code, stack, data); segment table (base, limit); external fragmentation → compaction.\n- **Segmented paging**: segments ko pages me todo.\n- **TLB**: associative memory; EAT = h(t + m) + (1 − h)(t + 2m).\n- **Thrashing**: degree of multiprogramming bahut zyada → CPU utilization girta; solution: working-set model, page-fault frequency, kam processes.\n- **Inverted page table**: ek entry per frame (memory bachat, search slow → hashing).","tips":"- Thrashing = \"busy paging, not working\".","sol":"Paging → IV, Segmentation → II, TLB → I, Thrashing → III.\n\n**Answer: (D) (A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Ch 9 (Main memory), Sec 10.6 (Thrashing)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-46":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"RE = P × C.","concept":"### Risk management\n- **Risk exposure** RE = P (probability) × C (cost/impact).\n- Risk types: project, technical, business (market, strategic, sales, management, budget).\n- Steps: identification → projection (estimation: probability + impact) → refinement → **RMMM** (Mitigation, Monitoring, Management).\n- Reactive vs proactive strategies.\n- Risk table sort by probability × impact; cutoff line.","tips":"- 0.6 × 50,000 = 30,000.","sol":"RE = 0.6 × 50,000 = **₹30,000**.\n\n**Answer: (A) ₹30,000**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Ch 35 (Risk management: risk projection, risk exposure, RMMM)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-47":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Quick sort worst = already sorted + bad pivot → n². Insertion sort sorted input pe n.","concept":"### Sorting complexities\n- Bubble/Selection/Insertion: O(n²) worst; insertion best O(n).\n- Merge: O(n log n) sab cases; O(n) space.\n- Quick: avg O(n log n), worst O(n²); randomized pivot se expected O(n log n).\n- Heap: O(n log n), in-place.\n- **Non-comparison**: counting O(n + k), radix O(d(n + k)), bucket avg O(n) (uniform input).\n- Comparison sort lower bound Ω(n log n).","tips":"- \"d(n + k)\" → radix; \"n + k\" → counting.","sol":"A → III, B → IV, C → I, D → II.\n\n**Answer: (A) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)**","refs":"- Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein — Introduction to Algorithms (CLRS), Ch 2, 7, 8 (Sorting in linear time)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-48":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"CNF me derivation length = 2n − 1 (n − 1 steps A → BC se, n steps A → a se).","concept":"### Normal forms\n- **CNF**: A → BC | a (S → ε allowed agar S RHS me na aaye). Derivation of length-n string: **2n − 1** steps; parse tree binary → height ≥ ⌈log₂ n⌉ + 1.\n- **GNF**: A → aα (α non-terminals). Derivation: **n** steps.\n- CNF conversion: ε-productions hatao → unit productions → useless symbols → long RHS todo, terminals replace.\n- **CYK** algorithm CNF grammar pe O(n³ |G|).","tips":"- GNF me 6 steps hote — option A trap.","sol":"2n − 1 = 2(6) − 1 = **11**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 11**","refs":"- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation, Sec 7.1.5 (Chomsky normal form)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-49":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"A = gᵃ mod p, B = gᵇ mod p, key = Bᵃ mod p = Aᵇ mod p.","concept":"### Diffie-Hellman\n1. Public: prime p, generator g.\n2. Alice: secret a, sends A = gᵃ mod p. Bob: secret b, sends B = gᵇ mod p.\n3. Key K = Bᵃ mod p = Aᵇ mod p = g^(ab) mod p.\n- Security: discrete logarithm problem.\n- Weakness: **man-in-the-middle** (authentication nahi) → signed DH / certificates.\n- Encryption nahi karta, sirf shared key banata.","tips":"- Dono taraf se calculate karke cross-check karo.","sol":"A = 2³ mod 11 = 8. B = 2⁴ mod 11 = 16 mod 11 = 5.\nK = Bᵃ = 5³ mod 11 = 125 mod 11 = **4**. Check: Aᵇ = 8⁴ = 4096 mod 11 = 4 ✓.\n\n**Answer: (D) 4**","refs":"- William Stallings — Cryptography and Network Security, Sec 10.1 (Diffie-Hellman key exchange)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-50":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Argument by argument: x ↔ a, f(y) ↔ f(b) ⇒ y ↔ b, z ↔ w (variable-variable).","concept":"### Unification\n- Substitution θ = {variable/term}. MGU = sabse general unifier (baaki unifiers isse specialise hote).\n- Rules: constant–constant same hon; variable → term (occurs check: x/f(x) nahi); function symbols aur arity match.\n- Different function symbols ya constants → fail (P(a) aur P(b)).\n- Resolution + unification → FOL theorem proving (refutation).","tips":"- Notation \"x/a\" = x ko a se replace. (C) me ulta likha hai.","sol":"x/a; f(y) = f(b) ⇒ y/b; z aur w dono variables ⇒ z/w (ya w/z).\nMGU = **{x/a, y/b, z/w}**.\n\n**Answer: (A) {x/a, y/b, z/w}**","refs":"- Russell & Norvig — Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach, Sec 9.2.2 (Unification)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-51":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Truth table me 2³ = 8 rows; har row ka output 0 ya 1 → 2⁸.","concept":"### Counting Boolean functions (n variables)\n- Total: 2^(2ⁿ) → n = 2: 16, n = 3: 256.\n- Self-dual functions: 2^(2^(n − 1)) → n = 3: 16.\n- Symmetric functions: 2^(n + 1).\n- Neutral functions (equal 0s and 1s): C(2ⁿ, 2^(n−1)).\n- Functional completeness: {NAND}, {NOR}, {AND, NOT}, {OR, NOT}.","tips":"- 2 variables ke 16 functions (AND, OR, XOR, NAND …) yaad rakho; formula se check.","sol":"2^(2³) = 2⁸ = **256**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 256**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Digital Design, Sec 2.6 (Other logic operations: 16 functions of two variables).\n- C. L. Liu — Elements of Discrete Mathematics, Ch 12 (Boolean algebras)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-52":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"p₀ = 2Δy − Δx (slope < 1 ke liye).","concept":"### Bresenham line (|m| < 1)\n- Δx = x₂ − x₁, Δy = y₂ − y₁.\n- p₀ = 2Δy − Δx.\n- pₖ < 0 → next point (xₖ + 1, yₖ), pₖ₊₁ = pₖ + 2Δy.\n- pₖ ≥ 0 → next point (xₖ + 1, yₖ + 1), pₖ₊₁ = pₖ + 2Δy − 2Δx.\n- Sirf integer addition/subtraction → DDA (floating point, rounding) se fast.\n- |m| > 1 → x aur y ke roles swap.","tips":"- Δx = 10, Δy = 6 → 12 − 10 = 2.","sol":"Δx = 10, Δy = 6 → p₀ = 2(6) − 10 = **2**. (p₀ ≥ 0 → next pixel (11, 6).)\n\n**Answer: (A) 2**","refs":"- Hearn & Baker — Computer Graphics (C version), Sec 3.2 (Line-drawing algorithms: Bresenham's line algorithm)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-53":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Roll-up = summary (upar), drill-down = detail (neeche), slice = ek dimension fix, pivot = ghumao.","concept":"### Data warehousing\n- Characteristics (Inmon): subject-oriented, integrated, time-variant, non-volatile.\n- **OLTP vs OLAP**: transactions (current, normalised) vs analysis (historical, denormalised).\n- Schemas: **star** (fact + denormalised dimensions), **snowflake** (normalised dimensions), **fact constellation** (multiple facts).\n- OLAP ops: roll-up, drill-down, **slice** (1 dimension fix), **dice** (2+ dimensions pe range select), pivot.\n- ROLAP, MOLAP, HOLAP. ETL: extract, transform, load.","tips":"- Dice = multiple dimensions ka sub-cube; slice = ek dimension.","sol":"Roll-up → III, Drill-down → IV, Slice → I, Pivot → II.\n\n**Answer: (B) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)**","refs":"- Han, Kamber, Pei — Data Mining: Concepts and Techniques, Sec 4.2 (Data cube and OLAP operations)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-54":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"0 = sirf striping (no safety), 1 = copy, 5 = single parity distributed, 6 = double parity.","concept":"### RAID levels\n- **RAID 0**: striping; best performance; koi fault tolerance nahi.\n- **RAID 1**: mirroring; 50% capacity; ek disk fail tolerate.\n- **RAID 2**: bit-level, Hamming code (obsolete).\n- **RAID 3**: byte-level striping + dedicated parity disk.\n- **RAID 4**: block-level + dedicated parity disk (parity disk bottleneck).\n- **RAID 5**: block-level + distributed parity; n disks → n − 1 capacity; 1 failure.\n- **RAID 6**: P + Q parity; n − 2 capacity; 2 failures.\n- **RAID 10 (1 + 0)**: mirror + stripe.","tips":"- Capacity: 6 disks RAID 5 → 5 disks ka data; RAID 6 → 4.","sol":"RAID 0 → III, RAID 1 → II, RAID 5 → I, RAID 6 → IV.\n\n**Answer: (C) (A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 11.8 (RAID structure)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-55":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"64^0.38 = 2^(6 × 0.38) = 2^2.28 ≈ 4.86.","concept":"### Basic COCOMO\n- E = a(KLOC)^b PM; D = c(E)^d months; average staff = E / D.\n- Organic: a 2.4, b 1.05, c 2.5, d 0.38.\n- Semi-detached: 3.0, 1.12, 2.5, 0.35.\n- Embedded: 3.6, 1.20, 2.5, 0.32.\n- Productivity = KLOC / E.","tips":"- Staff = 64 / 12.14 ≈ 5.3 log — follow-up sawaal.","sol":"D = 2.5 × 64^0.38 ≈ 2.5 × 4.86 ≈ **12.1 months**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 12 months**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Sec 33.6 (Empirical estimation models).\n- Barry Boehm — Software Engineering Economics."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-56":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"DP table banao: c[i][j] = c[i−1][j−1] + 1 (match) warna max(c[i−1][j], c[i][j−1]).","concept":"### LCS (dynamic programming)\n- c[i][j] = 0 agar i = 0 ya j = 0.\n- xᵢ = yⱼ → c[i−1][j−1] + 1; warna max(c[i−1][j], c[i][j−1]).\n- Time O(mn), space O(mn) (length ke liye O(min(m, n))).\n- Subsequence (contiguous nahi) vs substring (contiguous).\n- LCS(X, reverse X) → longest palindromic subsequence.","tips":"- Candidates check: \"CDB\", \"BDB\", \"CAB\" — sab length 3; \"CADB\"? A ke baad D chahiye X me — nahi milta.","sol":"Common subsequences of length 3: CDB (X: C, D, B; Y: C, D, B), BDB, CAB. Length 4 possible nahi (DP table se max 3).\nLCS length = **3**.\n\n**Answer: (D) 3**","refs":"- Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein — Introduction to Algorithms (CLRS), Sec 15.4 (Longest common subsequence)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-57":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Non-trivial = kuch RE languages ke paas property ho, kuch ke paas nahi. Aisi har (language-ki) property undecidable.","concept":"### Undecidability results\n- **Halting problem** (Turing): undecidable; RE hai, recursive nahi. Complement RE bhi nahi.\n- **Rice's theorem**: L(M) ki koi bhi non-trivial property undecidable — \"L(M) empty?\", \"finite?\", \"regular?\", \"contains ε?\".\n- Properties of the **machine** (states count, \"M 10 steps me ruke?\") Rice ke under nahi.\n- **PCP** undecidable; CFG ambiguity, CFG equivalence undecidable.\n- Reductions: A ≤m B, A undecidable ⇒ B undecidable.","tips":"- \"Language ki property\" + \"non-trivial\" = undecidable — reflex.","sol":"Rice's theorem: RE languages ki har non-trivial property undecidable.\n\n**Answer: (B) Every non-trivial property of the languages recognised by Turing machines is undecidable.**","refs":"- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation, Sec 9.3.3 (Rice's theorem)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-58":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"m + n − 1.","concept":"### Transportation problem\n- Balanced: total supply = total demand (warna dummy row/column, cost 0).\n- Initial BFS: **North-West Corner**, **Least Cost**, **Vogel's Approximation (VAM)** (penalty = do sabse kam costs ka difference; usually best start).\n- Optimality: **MODI (u-v) method** / stepping stone; uᵢ + vⱼ = cᵢⱼ basic cells pe; dᵢⱼ = cᵢⱼ − (uᵢ + vⱼ) ≥ 0 sab ke liye → optimal.\n- Allocations < m + n − 1 → **degenerate** (ε daalo).\n- **Assignment problem**: special case (n × n, har supply/demand 1) → Hungarian method.","tips":"- 3 + 4 − 1 = 6.","sol":"m + n − 1 = 3 + 4 − 1 = **6**.\n\n**Answer: (D) 6**","refs":"- Hamdy A. Taha — Operations Research: An Introduction, Ch 5 (Transportation model)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-59":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Pipeline clock = max stage delay + latch = 50 + 10 = 60 ns. Speedup → 140/60.","concept":"### Pipeline performance\n- Cycle time = max(stage delay) + latch/buffer delay.\n- n instructions, k stages: time = (k + n − 1) × cycle.\n- Speedup (n → ∞) = non-pipelined time per instruction / cycle time.\n- Ideal speedup = k (equal stages, no latch, no stalls).\n- Stalls: CPI = 1 + stall cycles per instruction.\n- Unequal stages → slowest stage pura pipeline slow karti.","tips":"- Latch delay jodna mat bhoolo (50 lete to 2.8 — option trap).","sol":"Cycle = 50 + 10 = 60 ns. Speedup ≈ 140 / 60 ≈ **2.33**.\n\n**Answer: (A) 2.33**","refs":"- Kai Hwang — Advanced Computer Architecture, Sec 6.1 (Linear pipeline processors).\n- William Stallings — Computer Organization and Architecture, Sec 14.4."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-60":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Upar → TOP bit 1; left → LEFT bit 1. Order TBRL → 1 0 0 1.","concept":"### Cohen-Sutherland line clipping\n- Har endpoint ka 4-bit outcode (TBRL ya kai books me LRBT — order padho).\n- Inside window → 0000.\n- **Trivially accept**: dono codes 0000 (OR = 0000).\n- **Trivially reject**: code1 AND code2 ≠ 0000 (dono same bahar wale side).\n- Warna intersection nikaalo, outside point replace karo, repeat.\n- Other algorithms: **Liang-Barsky** (parametric, faster), Cyrus-Beck (convex windows), Sutherland-Hodgman (polygon clipping), Weiler-Atherton (concave).","tips":"- Bit order question me diya hai — usi ke hisaab se likho.","sol":"TOP = 1, BOTTOM = 0, RIGHT = 0, LEFT = 1 → **1001**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 1001**","refs":"- Hearn & Baker — Computer Graphics (C version), Sec 6.7 (Line clipping: Cohen-Sutherland)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-61":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Hungarian = assignment; VAM = starting solution; MODI = optimality check; Simplex = general LP.","concept":"### Optimization methods (UGC syllabus)\n- **Simplex**: corner points pe iterate; slack (≤), surplus + artificial (≥); Big-M / Two-phase; degeneracy, unboundedness, infeasibility detection.\n- **Duality**: primal max ↔ dual min; dual variables = shadow prices.\n- **Transportation**: NWCR / LCM / VAM (initial), MODI / stepping stone (optimality).\n- **Assignment**: Hungarian — row reduction, column reduction, minimum lines cover; lines = n → optimal. Maximisation → (max − cᵢⱼ) se convert.","tips":"- Assignment ko transportation se bhi solve kar sakte, par Hungarian fast hai.","sol":"Hungarian → III, VAM → IV, MODI → I, Simplex → II.\n\n**Answer: (C) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)**","refs":"- Hamdy A. Taha — Operations Research: An Introduction, Ch 3 (Simplex), Ch 5 (Transportation and assignment models)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-62":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Chips = (256K / 32K) × (8 / 1).","concept":"### Memory organisation\n- Chips = (required words / chip words) × (required width / chip width).\n- Address lines = log₂(words): 256K → 18; chip 32K → 15.\n- Rows of chips = 8 → 3-to-8 decoder for chip select.\n- Data lines = word width.\n- Memory interleaving: low-order (consecutive words alag modules me — parallel access).","tips":"- 8 × 8 = 64; extra: decoder 3×8, address lines 18.","sol":"(256K / 32K) = 8 rows; (8 / 1) = 8 chips per row → 8 × 8 = **64** chips.\n\n**Answer: (C) 64**","refs":"- M. Morris Mano — Computer System Architecture, Sec 12.3 (Main memory: memory address map, chips)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-63":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Ellipsis (...) = catch-all; isko hamesha last handler rakho.","concept":"### Exception handling in C++\n- `try { … throw x; } catch (Type e) { … }`.\n- Handlers order me check hote; pehla matching chalta → specific pehle, `catch(...)` last.\n- Re-throw: handler ke andar `throw;`.\n- Exception object by reference catch karo (`catch (const std::exception& e)`) — slicing se bachav.\n- Stack unwinding: throw se catch tak local objects ke destructors chalte.\n- `noexcept` specifier (C++11).","tips":"- Java me catch-all = `catch (Exception e)`/`Throwable` — C++ me `...`.","sol":"`catch(...)` kisi bhi type ka exception pakadta hai.\n\n**Answer: (B) catch(...)**","refs":"- Bjarne Stroustrup — The C++ Programming Language, Ch 13 (Exception handling)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-64":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Agar Ti ka write(X) kisi younger transaction ke write ke baad aata hai (TS(Ti) < W-TS(X)), to Ti ka write \"outdated\" hai → bas ignore.","concept":"### Timestamp ordering (TO)\n- Har item X: R-TS(X), W-TS(X).\n- Ti read(X): TS(Ti) < W-TS(X) → rollback Ti.\n- Ti write(X): TS(Ti) < R-TS(X) → rollback; TS(Ti) < W-TS(X) → rollback (basic) / **ignore (Thomas)**.\n- Basic TO → conflict serializable, deadlock-free, starvation possible, cascading rollback possible.\n- Thomas write rule → **view serializable** (conflict serializable nahi) schedules allow.\n- Other: multiversion TO, optimistic (validation) concurrency control.","tips":"- \"Obsolete / outdated write ignore\" = Thomas.","sol":"Thomas' write rule outdated writes ko ignore karta hai, rollback nahi karta.\n\n**Answer: (A) Ignoring an obsolete write operation instead of rolling back the transaction**","refs":"- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 18.5 (Timestamp-based protocols, Thomas' write rule)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-65":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Har fork() processes double karta: 1 → 2 → 4 → 8. Sab printf chalate.","concept":"### fork()\n- Child me 0 return, parent me child ka PID, fail pe −1.\n- n sequential fork() → 2ⁿ processes (2ⁿ − 1 naye children).\n- `if (fork() == 0)` → sirf child branch.\n- `fork() && fork() || fork()` jaise expressions → short-circuit se count badalta (classic trap).\n- exec() family: process image replace; wait(): parent child ka intezaar; zombie (child khatam, parent ne wait nahi kiya), orphan (parent pehle khatam).","tips":"- \"\\n\" ke saath printf buffer flush ho jaata — duplicate output ka extra trap nahi.","sol":"3 forks → 2³ = 8 processes → \"Hello\" **8** baar.\n\n**Answer: (D) 8**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 3.3.1 (Process creation, fork())."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-66":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Product Owner = \"kya banana hai\" (backlog); Scrum Master = coach / rukawat hatane wala.","concept":"### Scrum\n- **Roles**: Product Owner, Scrum Master, Development Team (self-organising, cross-functional).\n- **Artifacts**: Product backlog, Sprint backlog, Increment; burndown chart.\n- **Events**: Sprint planning → Daily Scrum (3 questions: kal kya kiya, aaj kya karunga, kya rukawat) → Sprint review (demo) → Sprint retrospective (process improvement).\n- Velocity = story points per sprint.\n- Kanban: WIP limits, continuous flow (sprints nahi).","tips":"- Daily scrum me problem solve nahi karte, sirf sync.","sol":"A → II, B → IV, C → III, D → I.\n\n**Answer: (A) (A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)**","refs":"- Ken Schwaber & Jeff Sutherland — The Scrum Guide (2020).\n- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Sec 5.5 (Scrum)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-67":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Do hi parenthesizations: (AB)C aur A(BC). Dono compute karo.","concept":"### Matrix chain multiplication (DP)\n- p×q aur q×r multiply → p·q·r scalar multiplications.\n- m[i][j] = min over k (m[i][k] + m[k+1][j] + p_{i−1}·p_k·p_j); O(n³) time, O(n²) space.\n- n matrices ke parenthesizations = Catalan number C(n − 1) (n = 4 → 5).\n- Greedy (sabse bada dimension pehle khatam) hamesha optimal nahi.","tips":"- Beech wala chhota dimension (3) pehle \"khatam\" karo → (AB) first.","sol":"(AB)C = 5·10·3 + 5·3·12 = 150 + 180 = 330.\nA(BC) = 10·3·12 + 5·10·12 = 360 + 600 = 960.\nMinimum = **330**.\n\n**Answer: (D) 330**","refs":"- Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein — Introduction to Algorithms (CLRS), Sec 15.2 (Matrix-chain multiplication)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-68":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Pumping lemma regular languages ki **necessary** condition hai, sufficient nahi → sirf \"not regular\" prove karta.","concept":"### Pumping lemma (regular)\n- L regular ⇒ ∃ p: |w| ≥ p wali har w = xyz, |xy| ≤ p, |y| ≥ 1, xyⁱz ∈ L ∀ i ≥ 0.\n- Contradiction se: ek w chuno jisko kaise bhi todo, pump karne pe L se bahar.\n- Classic non-regular: aⁿbⁿ, palindromes, a^(n²), a^p (p prime), ww, equal 0s & 1s.\n- Regular prove karne ke liye: DFA/regex/regular grammar banao, ya closure properties, ya Myhill–Nerode (finite classes).","tips":"- Kuch non-regular languages pumping lemma satisfy kar leti hain → isse \"regular\" prove nahi hota.","sol":"Pumping lemma → language **non-regular** prove karne ka tool.\n\n**Answer: (C) prove that a given language is not regular**","refs":"- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation, Sec 4.1 (Pumping lemma for regular languages)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-69":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Pure: S = Ge^(−2G), max 1/(2e) at G = ½. Slotted: S = Ge^(−G), max 1/e at G = 1.","concept":"### Random access protocols\n- **Pure ALOHA**: vulnerable time 2·Tfr; S = G·e^(−2G); S_max = 0.184.\n- **Slotted ALOHA**: vulnerable time Tfr; S = G·e^(−G); S_max = 0.368.\n- **CSMA** (1-persistent, non-persistent, p-persistent); **CSMA/CD** (Ethernet), **CSMA/CA** (Wi-Fi: IFS, contention window, ACK).\n- Controlled access: reservation, polling, token passing. Channelization: FDMA, TDMA, CDMA.","tips":"- Slotted = double throughput, double load.","sol":"A → 0.184 (III), B → 0.368 (I), C → 0.5 (IV), D → 1 (II).\n\n**Answer: (D) (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Sec 12.1 (Random access: ALOHA, CSMA/CD, CSMA/CA)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-70":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Internal state + world model = model-based.","concept":"### Agent types (Russell & Norvig)\n- **Simple reflex**: sirf current percept, condition–action rules; fully observable me hi theek.\n- **Model-based reflex**: internal state (world model) → partially observable handle.\n- **Goal-based**: goals + search/planning.\n- **Utility-based**: utility function (kitna achha) → trade-offs, uncertainty.\n- **Learning agent**: performance element, critic, learning element, problem generator.\n- **PEAS**: Performance, Environment, Actuators, Sensors (taxi: safe/fast; roads; steering; cameras).\n- Environment: fully/partially observable, deterministic/stochastic, episodic/sequential, static/dynamic, discrete/continuous, single/multi-agent.","tips":"- \"Internal state\" keyword = model-based.","sol":"Internal state + world model → **model-based reflex agent**.\n\n**Answer: (B) Model-based reflex agent**","refs":"- Russell & Norvig — Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach, Sec 2.4 (The structure of agents)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-71":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Basis → hypothesis → inductive step → conclusion.","concept":"### Mathematical induction\n- **Weak**: P(1) ∧ (P(k) → P(k + 1)) ⇒ ∀n P(n).\n- **Strong**: P(1), …, P(k) sab maan ke P(k + 1).\n- **Well-ordering principle** ke equivalent.\n- Examples: 1 + 2 + … + n = n(n + 1)/2; 2ⁿ > n; n³ − n divisible by 6; har n ≥ 2 primes ka product (strong).\n- Structural induction: recursively defined sets/trees ke liye.","tips":"- Basis bhoolna = proof galat (e.g. \"sab horses same colour\" fallacy).","sol":"A (basis) → B (hypothesis) → C (inductive step) → D (conclusion).\n\n**Answer: (C) (A), (B), (C), (D)**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 5.1–5.2 (Mathematical and strong induction)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-72":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"RISC: simple, fixed-length, load/store, hardwired, many registers, pipelining-friendly. CISC: complex instructions, many addressing modes, microprogrammed.","concept":"### RISC vs CISC\n- **RISC**: kam instructions, fixed format, 1 instruction ≈ 1 cycle, load/store, bade register files (register windows — SPARC), hardwired control, compiler pe zyada load. Examples: ARM, MIPS, RISC-V, SPARC.\n- **CISC**: bahut instructions, variable length, memory-to-memory operations, many addressing modes, microprogrammed control. Examples: x86, VAX.\n- Modern x86: CISC instructions ko micro-ops (RISC-like) me translate karte.","tips":"- Addressing modes zyada = CISC; isliye (C) ulta.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✗, (D) ✗ (hardwired), (E) ✓.\n\n**Answer: (A) (A), (B) and (E) Only**","refs":"- William Stallings — Computer Organization and Architecture, Ch 15 (Reduced instruction set computers).\n- M. Morris Mano — Computer System Architecture, Sec 8.8 (RISC)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-73":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"p₀ = 1 − r (5/4 − r ka integer version).","concept":"### Midpoint circle algorithm\n- Start (0, r); octant x = 0 se x = y tak; 8-way symmetry.\n- p₀ = 5/4 − r ≈ **1 − r**.\n- pₖ < 0 → next (xₖ + 1, yₖ), pₖ₊₁ = pₖ + 2xₖ₊₁ + 1.\n- pₖ ≥ 0 → next (xₖ + 1, yₖ − 1), pₖ₊₁ = pₖ + 2xₖ₊₁ + 1 − 2yₖ₊₁.\n- Ellipse: midpoint ellipse, do regions.\n- Fill: scan-line, boundary fill, flood fill (4/8-connected).","tips":"- r = 10 → −9; r = 8 → −7.","sol":"p₀ = 1 − 8 = **−7**. (p₀ < 0 → next pixel (1, 8).)\n\n**Answer: (C) −7**","refs":"- Hearn & Baker — Computer Graphics (C version), Sec 3.5 (Midpoint circle algorithm)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-74":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"bfr = ⌊4096/200⌋ = 20; blocks b = 50000/20 = 2500; binary search ≈ ⌈log₂ b⌉.","concept":"### File access cost (Elmasri style)\n- Blocking factor bfr = ⌊B / R⌋ (unspanned).\n- Blocks b = ⌈r / bfr⌉.\n- Linear search: average b/2, worst b.\n- Binary search (ordered file): ⌈log₂ b⌉.\n- Primary index: ⌈log₂ bᵢ⌉ + 1 (index blocks pe binary search + 1 data block).\n- Multilevel index: levels t = ⌈log_fo bᵢ⌉; accesses = t + 1.","tips":"- log₂ 2048 = 11, log₂ 4096 = 12 → 2500 beech me → 12.","sol":"bfr = 20 → b = 2500 → ⌈log₂ 2500⌉ = ⌈11.29⌉ = **12**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 12**","refs":"- Elmasri & Navathe — Fundamentals of Database Systems, Sec 16.4–16.7 (Files of ordered records, binary search cost), Sec 17.1."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-75":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Use case = actors + functionality; sequence = time order; class = static; statechart = states.","concept":"### UML diagrams\n- **Structural**: class, object, component, deployment, package, composite structure.\n- **Behavioural**: use case, activity, state machine; **interaction**: sequence, communication (collaboration), timing, interaction overview.\n- Class relationships: association, aggregation (hollow diamond, \"has-a\" weak), composition (filled diamond, strong lifetime), generalization (inheritance, hollow triangle), dependency (dashed).\n- Use case relationships: <<include>> (hamesha), <<extend>> (conditional).","tips":"- Sequence aur communication diagrams \"isomorphic\" (same info, alag view).","sol":"A → III, B → I, C → IV, D → II.\n\n**Answer: (A) (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)**","refs":"- Grady Booch, James Rumbaugh, Ivar Jacobson — The Unified Modeling Language User Guide.\n- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Appendix 1 (UML)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-76":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"BFS → queue; DFS → stack/recursion. Adjacency matrix pe har vertex ki row scan → O(V²).","concept":"### BFS / DFS\n- **BFS**: queue; level order; unweighted shortest path; bipartite check; O(V + E) (list), O(V²) (matrix).\n- **DFS**: stack/recursion; discovery/finish times; edge types (tree, back, forward, cross); **back edge ⇔ cycle** (directed); topological sort (finish time decreasing); SCC (Kosaraju, Tarjan); articulation points, bridges.\n- Topological sort only for DAG; Kahn's algorithm (in-degree 0 queue).","tips":"- Representation ke saath complexity badalti — matrix = V².","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✓, (D) ✗ (queue), (E) ✗ (O(V²)).\n\n**Answer: (C) (A), (B) and (C) Only**","refs":"- Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein — Introduction to Algorithms (CLRS), Ch 22 (Elementary graph algorithms)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-77":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Do tareeke: (id + id) * id aur id + (id * id).","concept":"### Ambiguity\n- Ek string ke 2+ parse trees (ya leftmost derivations) → ambiguous grammar.\n- Remove: precedence + associativity levels: E → E + T | T, T → T * F | F, F → (E) | id.\n- **Inherently ambiguous language**: har grammar ambiguous ({aⁱbʲcᵏ | i = j or j = k}).\n- Ambiguity check undecidable (general CFG).\n- n operators (same operator, no precedence) → parse trees = Catalan number C(n): 2 operators → 2, 3 → 5.","tips":"- 3 operators ho (id + id * id − id) → 5 trees.","sol":"Parse trees: E + (E * E) aur (E + E) * E → **2**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 2**","refs":"- Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman — Compilers (Dragon Book), Sec 4.2.5 (Ambiguity), Sec 4.3.2 (Eliminating ambiguity)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-78":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Har path ki total duration nikaalo; sabse lamba path = critical path = project duration.","concept":"### CPM / PERT\n- **Critical path** = start se end tak sabse lamba path; iski activities me slack 0.\n- Forward pass: ES, EF = ES + duration; backward pass: LF, LS = LF − duration.\n- **Slack (float)** = LS − ES = LF − EF.\n- PERT: expected time tₑ = (a + 4m + b)/6; variance ((b − a)/6)².\n- Gantt chart: schedule bars; network diagram: dependencies.","tips":"- C ka slack = 12 − 10 = 2 din (A–C–D path 10 ka hai).","sol":"Paths: A–B–D = 3 + 4 + 5 = **12**; A–C–D = 10; A–C–E = 7.\nMinimum duration = 12 days; critical path **A–B–D**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 12 days; A–B–D**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Sec 34.5 (Scheduling: CPM, PERT, timeline charts).\n- Hamdy A. Taha — Operations Research: An Introduction, Ch 6 (Network models: CPM and PERT)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-79":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"T(1,2) = max( min(R11, S12), min(R12, S22) ).","concept":"### Fuzzy relations\n- **Max-min composition**: T(x, z) = max_y min(R(x, y), S(y, z)) — matrix multiplication jaisa, × ki jagah min, + ki jagah max.\n- **Max-product**: max_y R(x, y)·S(y, z).\n- Fuzzy relation properties: reflexive, symmetric, transitive (max-min).\n- Fuzzy inference (Mamdani): fuzzification → rule evaluation → aggregation → **defuzzification** (centroid, mean of maximum, bisector).\n- Sugeno: rule outputs functions (weighted average).","tips":"- Row 1 of R × column 2 of S.","sol":"min(0.7, 0.6) = 0.6; min(0.5, 0.7) = 0.5 → max = **0.6**.\n\n**Answer: (D) 0.6**","refs":"- Timothy J. Ross — Fuzzy Logic with Engineering Applications, Ch 3 (Classical and fuzzy relations, composition)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-80":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"aₙ = aₙ₋₁ + aₙ₋₂ (string 1 se shuru → aₙ₋₁; \"01\" se shuru → aₙ₋₂); a₁ = 2, a₂ = 3.","concept":"### Recurrence relations in counting\n- No two consecutive 0s: aₙ = aₙ₋₁ + aₙ₋₂ → Fibonacci shifted: aₙ = F(n + 2).\n- Tower of Hanoi: Hₙ = 2Hₙ₋₁ + 1 = 2ⁿ − 1.\n- Derangements: Dₙ = (n − 1)(Dₙ₋₁ + Dₙ₋₂).\n- Linear homogeneous recurrence: characteristic equation; repeated roots → (c₁ + c₂n)rⁿ.\n- Generating functions.","tips":"- 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21 → n = 6 pe 21.","sol":"a₁ = 2, a₂ = 3, a₃ = 5, a₄ = 8, a₅ = 13, a₆ = **21**.\n\n**Answer: (B) 21**","refs":"- Kenneth Rosen — Discrete Mathematics and Its Applications, Sec 8.1 (Applications of recurrence relations, Example 3)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-81":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Programmed I/O me CPU busy-wait karta (sabse zyada); I/O processor apne programs khud chalata (sabse kam).","concept":"### I/O techniques\n- **Programmed I/O**: CPU status bar-bar check (polling) → time waste.\n- **Interrupt-driven**: device ready hone par interrupt; har word/byte pe CPU involve.\n- **DMA**: block transfer; CPU sirf shuru (setup) aur end (interrupt) me; cycle stealing.\n- **I/O processor / channel**: apna instruction set (channel programs); CPU sirf command deta — selector, multiplexer, block-multiplexer channels.","tips":"- Order = evolution of I/O: programmed → interrupt → DMA → channel.","sol":"Programmed I/O (B) > Interrupt (C) > DMA (A) > I/O processor (D).\n\n**Answer: (D) (B), (C), (A), (D)**","refs":"- William Stallings — Computer Organization and Architecture, Ch 7 (Input/output: programmed, interrupt-driven, DMA, I/O channels)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-82":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"<ol> = ordered (numbered), <ul> = unordered. HTML case-insensitive (XML/XHTML case-sensitive).","concept":"### HTML essentials\n- Structure: `<!DOCTYPE html>`, `<html>`, `<head>` (title, meta, link, script), `<body>`.\n- Void elements: `<img>`, `<br>`, `<hr>`, `<input>`, `<meta>`, `<link>`.\n- Lists: `<ol>` (ordered), `<ul>` (unordered), `<dl>` (description).\n- Tables: `<table>`, `<tr>`, `<th>`, `<td>`, `rowspan`, `colspan`.\n- Forms: `<form action method>`, GET (URL me data) vs POST (body me).\n- CSS: inline, internal (`<style>`), external (`<link>`); specificity: inline > id > class > element.\n- JavaScript: client-side; DOM manipulation; `document.getElementById`.","tips":"- \"o\" = ordered, \"u\" = unordered.","sol":"(A) ✓, (B) ✓, (C) ✗, (D) ✓, (E) ✗.\n\n**Answer: (D) (A), (B) and (D) Only**","refs":"- W3C / WHATWG — HTML Living Standard (elements: a, img, ol, table).\n- Deitel & Deitel — Internet & World Wide Web: How to Program, Ch 2–3 (HTML5)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-83":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Pehle table (FROM), phir rows filter (WHERE), phir groups (GROUP BY), phir groups filter (HAVING), phir columns (SELECT), last me ORDER BY.","concept":"### Logical query processing order\nFROM (+ JOIN) → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → DISTINCT → ORDER BY → LIMIT/OFFSET.\n- Isiliye SELECT me diya alias WHERE me use nahi hota (par ORDER BY me ho jaata).\n- WHERE me aggregate nahi; HAVING me aggregate.\n- Written order: SELECT … FROM … WHERE … GROUP BY … HAVING … ORDER BY.","tips":"- Written order aur evaluation order alag hain — option A written order hai (trap).","sol":"FROM (B) → WHERE (D) → GROUP BY (E) → HAVING (C) → SELECT (A).\n\n**Answer: (C) (B), (D), (E), (C), (A)**","refs":"- Korth — Database System Concepts, Sec 3.7 (Aggregate functions, HAVING), Sec 3.3.\n- Itzik Ben-Gan — T-SQL Fundamentals, Ch 2 (Logical query processing)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-84":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Revision = software ko **badalna** (fix, change, test). Transition = naye environment me le jaana.","concept":"### McCall's quality factors (1977)\n- **Product operation**: correctness, reliability, efficiency, integrity, usability.\n- **Product revision**: maintainability, flexibility, testability.\n- **Product transition**: portability, reusability, interoperability.\n### ISO 9126 (6 characteristics)\nFunctionality, reliability, usability, efficiency, maintainability, portability (ISO 25010 adds security, compatibility).","tips":"- \"PORT\" factors (portability, reusability, interoperability) = transition.","sol":"Maintainability, flexibility, testability → **product revision**.\n\n**Answer: (B) Product revision**","refs":"- Pressman — Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach, Sec 19.2 (McCall's quality factors, ISO 9126)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-85":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Backtracking se: column positions (2, 4, 1, 3) aur uska mirror (3, 1, 4, 2).","concept":"### Backtracking\n- State-space tree; promising na ho to prune (bounding function).\n- **N-Queens**: solutions — n = 4: 2, n = 5: 10, n = 6: 4, n = 8: 92 (12 fundamentally distinct). n = 2, 3 → 0.\n- Other backtracking problems: sum of subsets, graph colouring (m-colouring), Hamiltonian cycle.\n- **Branch and bound**: optimisation (0/1 knapsack, TSP, job sequencing) with bounds; FIFO / LIFO / least-cost search.","tips":"- n = 6 ke 4 solutions — kam lagte hain par sahi hain (trap ke liye yaad).","sol":"4-Queens ke solutions: (2, 4, 1, 3) aur (3, 1, 4, 2) → **2**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 2**","refs":"- Horowitz, Sahni, Rajasekaran — Fundamentals of Computer Algorithms, Sec 7.2 (The 8-queens problem)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-86":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"A → Aα | β ⇒ A → βA', A' → αA' | ε. Yahan α = + T, β = T.","concept":"### Left recursion & left factoring\n- Top-down (LL) parsers left recursion pe infinite loop me jaate → hatana zaroori.\n- **Immediate**: A → Aα₁ | … | β₁ | … ⇒ A → β₁A' | …, A' → α₁A' | … | ε.\n- Indirect left recursion: substitution order se hatao.\n- **Left factoring**: A → αβ₁ | αβ₂ ⇒ A → αA', A' → β₁ | β₂ (common prefix).\n- LL(1) condition: har A → α | β ke liye FIRST(α) ∩ FIRST(β) = ∅; agar β ⇒* ε to FIRST(α) ∩ FOLLOW(A) = ∅.","tips":"- Option C right recursive hai par associativity badal deta aur grammar non-LL(1) (common prefix T).","sol":"E → E + T | T: α = \"+ T\", β = \"T\" → **E → T E', E' → + T E' | ε**.\n\n**Answer: (A) E → T E',  E' → + T E' | ε**","refs":"- Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman — Compilers (Dragon Book), Sec 4.3.3 (Elimination of left recursion), Sec 4.3.4 (Left factoring)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-87":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Sigmoid 0–1, tanh −1 se 1, ReLU negative ko 0, step 0/1.","concept":"### Activation functions\n- **Sigmoid** σ(x) = 1/(1 + e^(−x)); σ' = σ(1 − σ) (max 0.25 → vanishing gradient).\n- **tanh** = 2σ(2x) − 1; zero-centred.\n- **ReLU** max(0, x): fast, sparse; \"dying ReLU\" problem → Leaky ReLU.\n- **Softmax**: multi-class output probabilities (sum 1).\n- **Step / sign**: perceptron (non-differentiable → backprop me nahi).","tips":"- Binary classification output → sigmoid; multi-class → softmax; hidden layers → ReLU.","sol":"Sigmoid → IV, tanh → II, ReLU → I, Step → III.\n\n**Answer: (D) (A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)**","refs":"- Goodfellow, Bengio, Courville — Deep Learning, Sec 6.3 (Hidden units: ReLU, sigmoid, tanh).\n- Simon Haykin — Neural Networks and Learning Machines, Sec 4.6."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-88":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Naive: t1 = a + b, t2 = a + b, t3 = t1 * t2, t4 = t3 − c, x = t4. DAG me (a + b) ek baar.","concept":"### Intermediate code\n- **Three-address code (TAC)**: x = y op z; forms — **quadruples** (op, arg1, arg2, result), **triples** (result = position), **indirect triples** (list of pointers, reordering easy).\n- **DAG** of basic block: common subexpressions share nodes.\n- Other IRs: syntax tree, postfix (a b + a b + * c −), SSA form (har variable ek baar assign).\n- Backpatching: boolean expressions / jumps ke liye.","tips":"- Postfix of the same: a b + a b + * c −.","sol":"Without optimization: t1 = a + b; t2 = a + b; t3 = t1 * t2; t4 = t3 − c; x = t4 → **5**.\nWith DAG (CSE): t1 = a + b; t2 = t1 * t1; t3 = t2 − c; x = t3 → **4**.\n\n**Answer: (A) 5 and 4**","refs":"- Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman — Compilers (Dragon Book), Sec 6.1 (DAGs for expressions), Sec 6.2 (Three-address code), Sec 8.5."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-89":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Static: f jahan **likha** hai (global x = 10). Dynamic: f jahan se **call** hua (g ka local x = 20).","concept":"### Scoping\n- **Static (lexical)**: free variable ka binding program text ke nesting se (C, C++, Java, Pascal); access link / display.\n- **Dynamic**: run-time call chain me sabse recent binding (early LISP, Perl `local`, shell).\n- Activation record fields: return value, actual parameters, control link (dynamic link), access link (static link), saved machine status, local data, temporaries.\n- Storage allocation: static, stack (activation records), heap (dynamic data).","tips":"- Static → \"definition ka environment\"; dynamic → \"caller ka environment\".","sol":"Static: f ke andar x → global x = 10. Dynamic: call chain main → g → f; g ka x = 20.\nOutput: **10 and 20**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 10 and 20**","refs":"- Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman — Compilers (Dragon Book), Sec 1.6.3 (Static scope and block structure), Sec 7.3 (Access to nonlocal data).\n- Robert W. Sebesta — Concepts of Programming Languages, Sec 5.8 (Static and dynamic scope)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-90":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"CNF + bottom-up DP table (triangle) = CYK.","concept":"### Parsing in NLP\n- **CYK**: CNF grammar; table V[i, j] = non-terminals deriving substring; O(n³·|G|).\n- **Earley**: any CFG; O(n³) worst, O(n²) unambiguous, O(n) for many grammars; top-down with chart.\n- **Chart parsing**, probabilistic CFG (PCFG) → most likely parse (Viterbi-style CYK).\n- **Dependency parsing** vs constituency parsing.\n- Other NLP: POS tagging (HMM, Viterbi), n-gram models, word embeddings, transformers.","tips":"- \"CNF\" + \"O(n³)\" + \"DP\" → CYK.","sol":"Ye CYK algorithm ka description hai.\n\n**Answer: (C) CYK (Cocke–Younger–Kasami) algorithm**","refs":"- Jurafsky & Martin — Speech and Language Processing, Ch 13 (Constituency parsing: CKY).\n- Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman — Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation, Sec 7.4.4."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-91":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Need = Max − Allocation.","concept":"### Banker's data structures\n- **Available** (m), **Max** (n × m), **Allocation** (n × m), **Need = Max − Allocation**.\n- Available = Total − Σ Allocation. Check: Σ Allocation = (6, 4, 5); Total (9, 5, 8) − (6, 4, 5) = (3, 1, 3) = Available ✓.","tips":"- Element-wise subtraction; koi negative aaye to data galat.","sol":"Need(P2) = (7, 0, 2) − (1, 0, 1) = **(6, 0, 1)**.\n\n**Answer: (B) (6, 0, 1)**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 8.6.3 (Banker's algorithm)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-92":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Need: P0 (3,2,1), P1 (1,1,1), P2 (6,0,1), P3 (1,1,2), P4 (2,1,2). Available (3,1,3). Har step pe Need ≤ Work check, phir Work += Allocation.","concept":"### Safety algorithm\n1. Work = Available; Finish[i] = false.\n2. Aisa i dhoondho: Finish[i] = false aur Need_i ≤ Work.\n3. Work = Work + Allocation_i; Finish[i] = true; step 2 pe wapas.\n4. Sab Finish true → safe.\n- Ek se zyada safe sequences ho sakti hain; ek bhi mil jaaye to state safe.","tips":"- Pehla element hi check karo — P0 (B = 2 > 1) aur P2 (A = 6 > 3) pehle nahi chal sakte.","sol":"Need: P0 (3,2,1), P1 (1,1,1), P2 (6,0,1), P3 (1,1,2), P4 (2,1,2).\n- (A) P0 first: B 2 > 1 ✗.\n- (B) P2 first: A 6 > 3 ✗.\n- (D) P4 ✓ → Work (5,1,4); P0: B 2 > 1 ✗.\n- (C) P1 ✓ → (5,2,4); P3 ✓ → (5,4,4); P4 ✓ → (7,4,5); P0 ✓ → (8,5,7); P2 ✓ → (9,5,8). **Safe**.\n\n**Answer: (C) ⟨P1, P3, P4, P0, P2⟩**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 8.6.3.1 (Safety algorithm)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-93":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Request ≤ Need ✓, ≤ Available ✓. Pretend allocate → Available (2, 0, 3). Ab safety check karo.","concept":"### Resource-request algorithm\n1. Request ≤ Need? Nahi → error.\n2. Request ≤ Available? Nahi → wait.\n3. Pretend allocate; safety algorithm.\n4. Safe → grant; unsafe → rollback, process wait.\n- Unsafe ≠ deadlock, par Banker's risk nahi leta.","tips":"- Available ka B = 0 ho gaya → jiska bhi B need > 0 hai wo pehle nahi chal sakta.","sol":"After pretend: Available = (2, 0, 3); P0 Allocation = (2, 2, 2), Need = (2, 1, 1).\nNeeds: P0 (2,1,1), P1 (1,1,1), P2 (6,0,1), P3 (1,1,2), P4 (2,1,2).\nB = 0 → sirf P2 (B need 0) — par A 6 > 2. Koi process start nahi kar sakta → **unsafe** → P0 must wait.\n\n**Answer: (D) Make P0 wait, because although the resources are available, granting them would leave the system in an unsafe state**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 8.6.3.2 (Resource-request algorithm)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-94":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Request (0,1,0) ≤ Need (1,1,2) ✓, ≤ Available (3,1,3) ✓. New Available (3,0,3), P3 Need (1,0,2).","concept":"### After a grant\n- Available, Allocation_i, Need_i teeno update.\n- Phir safety algorithm; safe sequence mil gayi to grant pakka.\n- Dhyan: Need ab bhi Max − Allocation hi hai (Max nahi badalta).","tips":"- P3 ka Need ab (1, 0, 2) — B me 0, isliye Available B = 0 hone par bhi P3 chal sakta.","sol":"Available (3,0,3). P3 Need (1,0,2) ≤ → Work (3,3,3) [P3 Allocation ab (0,3,0)];\nP0 (3,2,1) ≤ → (4,4,5); P1 (1,1,1) → (6,5,6); P2 (6,0,1) → (7,5,7); P4 (2,1,2) → (9,5,8).\n⟨P3, P0, P1, P2, P4⟩ safe → **request granted**.\n\n**Answer: (A) The request is granted; afterwards ⟨P3, P0, P1, P2, P4⟩ is a safe sequence**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 8.6.3."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-95":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"First process ka Need ≤ Available (3, 1, 3) hona chahiye.","concept":"### Quick test for the first process\n- Need_i ≤ Available → wo pehle chal sakta (aur finish karke resources lauta dega).\n- Phir dekhna padta poori sequence banti hai ya nahi — yahan Q92 me P1 se shuru safe sequence mil chuki; P3 aur P4 se bhi banti hain.","tips":"- B column me Available sirf 1 → P0 (B need 2) out.","sol":"Available (3, 1, 3):\n- P0 (3,2,1): B 2 > 1 ✗\n- P1 (1,1,1) ✓ (e.g. ⟨P1, P3, P4, P0, P2⟩)\n- P2 (6,0,1): A 6 > 3 ✗\n- P3 (1,1,2) ✓ (e.g. ⟨P3, P1, P4, P0, P2⟩)\n- P4 (2,1,2) ✓ (e.g. ⟨P4, P1, P3, P0, P2⟩)\n→ **P1, P3, P4**.\n\n**Answer: (B) (B), (D) and (E) Only**","refs":"- Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne — Operating System Concepts, Sec 8.6.3.1."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-96":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Last octet: 130 AND 192 = 128.","concept":"### Network & broadcast addresses\n- Network = IP AND mask.\n- Broadcast = network OR (NOT mask) → host bits sab 1.\n- Block size (interesting octet) = 256 − mask value = 256 − 192 = 64 → subnets .0, .64, .128, .192.\n- First usable = network + 1, last usable = broadcast − 1.","tips":"- 130 kis block me? 128–191.","sol":"130 = 10000010; 192 = 11000000 → AND = 10000000 = 128.\nNetwork = **172.16.45.128**.\n\n**Answer: (C) 172.16.45.128**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Sec 18.4 (Subnetting, network address)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-97":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Block 128–191 → last address 191.","concept":"### Broadcast types\n- **Directed broadcast**: subnet ke sab hosts (host bits all 1s) — routers forward kar sakte (mostly disabled).\n- **Limited broadcast**: 255.255.255.255 — sirf local network.\n- Network address: host bits all 0s — host ko assign nahi.","tips":"- Next subnet 192 se shuru → broadcast = 192 − 1 = 191.","sol":"Network 172.16.45.128, 6 host bits → broadcast = 128 + 63 = **172.16.45.191**.\n\n**Answer: (A) 172.16.45.191**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Sec 18.4 (Special addresses)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-98":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Host bits = 6 → 2⁶ − 2.","concept":"### Hosts per subnet\n- /26 → host bits 6 → 2⁶ − 2 = 62.\n- /25 → 126, /27 → 30, /28 → 14, /29 → 6, /30 → 2, /31 → point-to-point (RFC 3021, 2 addresses).\n- −2: network address aur broadcast address.","tips":"- 64 total addresses, 62 usable.","sol":"2⁶ − 2 = **62**.\n\n**Answer: (D) 62**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Sec 18.4 (Subnetting)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-99":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"First octet 172 → 128–191 → Class B → default /16.","concept":"### Classful defaults\n- Class A (1–126): /8 → 255.0.0.0\n- Class B (128–191): /16 → 255.255.0.0\n- Class C (192–223): /24 → 255.255.255.0\n- 172.16.0.0 – 172.31.255.255 = private Class B range (RFC 1918).","tips":"- Default mask class ka hota hai, subnet mask nahi — (D) me mix kar diya hai.","sol":"172 → **Class B**, default mask **255.255.0.0**. (Ye private address range 172.16/12 me bhi aata hai.)\n\n**Answer: (B) Class B, 255.255.0.0**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Sec 18.4 (Classful addressing)."},"ugc-exp-2026-12-s2-100":{"syl":{"s":"in","why":""},"hint":"Default /16, ab /26 → subnet bits = 10.","concept":"### Subnets count\n- Subnet bits s = new prefix − default prefix.\n- Subnets = 2^s (modern practice; purane classful rule me 2^s − 2, jab all-0s / all-1s subnets use nahi hote the — passage me sab usable diye hain).\n- Total hosts check: 1024 × 62 = 63,488 usable hosts.","tips":"- 26 − 16 = 10 → 2¹⁰.","sol":"Subnet bits = 26 − 16 = 10 → 2¹⁰ = **1024** subnets.\n\n**Answer: (C) 1024**","refs":"- Forouzan — Data Communications and Networking, Sec 18.4 (Subnetting a classful network)."}});
